# Rig evaluation architecture

Read `maya_tools/docs/rig_performance_plan.md` before restructuring a rig.

- Keep dependencies explicit and directional: controls -> systems -> skeleton/
  primary deformation -> correctives -> final detail. Final detail must not
  drive the same controls/joints/deformation from which it was computed.
- Use Python for builds, authoring, audits and offline baking. Runtime deformation
  must use suitable native nodes or validated compiled implementations. Do not
  add per-frame Python callbacks, scriptJobs or expressions with hidden getAttr.
- C++ alone does not guarantee parallelism or GPU execution. Declare scheduling
  only after thread-safety tests; GPU deformation needs its own implementation.
  Audit Parallel, Serial, GloballySerial and Untrusted separately.
- Prefer compact matrix networks when they reduce measured work. Preserve local
  animation channels, offsets, jointOrient, rotate order, mirrored/nonuniform
  scale and segmentScaleCompensate. Validate IK/FK matching and space switching.
- For offsetParentMatrix parent-space conversion, use the actual parent's inverse
  world matrix when required. Do not feed a driven node's dependent inverse back
  into itself or blindly copy the study's parentInverseMatrix example.
- Share common matrix/space calculations and batch pins per driver surface where
  possible. Resolve static nearest-point projections and rest data at build time.
- Profile existing motionPath, IK, decomposeMatrix and repeated utility chains
  before replacements. Constraint count alone does not establish cycles; keep
  required pole-vector/orient constraints unless a validated replacement wins.
- Audit source geometry consumers, including Bifrost, uvPin, proximityWrap,
  follicles and live targets. A hidden mesh needed downstream still evaluates.
  uvPin by name alone does not guarantee absence of GPU read-back.
- Separate lightweight attachment/collision drivers from final display detail.
  Verify which branch stays on GPU; do not assume every native node is supported.
- Keep topology stable. Preserve valid Orig shapes, bind/rest geometry, targets,
  namespaces and ownership links. Clean only proven redundant owned nodes in a
  versioned copy; never delete all history from a skinned production mesh.
- Stacked skinClusters, DQ, blendShapes, RBF and wraps are allowed when validated.
  Prune/bake/consolidate only measured costly layers with held-out pose tests.
  Do not reduce approved weights or change skinning methods solely for speed.
- Store corrective deltas sparsely when supported; avoid full live duplicate
  meshes for stored sculpts. Preserve target aliases/indices and deformer order.
  Keep topologyCheck unless a verified pipeline contract justifies disabling it.
- Use typed/semantic lookups and Maya API bulk arrays for large data; avoid
  per-vertex cmds loops and repeated whole-scene scans in interactive callbacks.
- Integrate reports and switches into existing Test Rig/Framework/asset workflows.
  Keep profiling and validation overhead outside normal animation evaluation.
